.TH BEAT 1
.SH NAME
beat \- drum sequencer and pattern editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B beat
[
.I pattern
]
.br
.B beat -e
[
.B -p
]
.I pattern
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Beat
reads a bar of four quarter notes from
.I pattern
or standard input and writes a timed OPL3 register stream to standard output.
Play the bar or record stereo PCM with
.IR opl3 (1):
.EX
beat rhythm | games/opl3 >/dev/audio
beat rhythm | games/opl3 >bar.pcm
.EE
.PP
A pattern has a tempo in quarter notes per minute (30 to 300)
and four rows of sixteen steps.
An
.B x
plays a hit and a dot is a rest.
Lines may appear in any order.
Blank lines and text following
.B #
are ignored.
.EX
bpm   120
kick  x...x...x...x...
snare ....x.......x...
hat   x.x.x.x.x.x.x.x.
clap  ..............x.
.EE
.PP
.B -e
opens the pattern in a window and creates it with the rhythm above
if it does not exist.
Click a cell to toggle a hit.
Space starts or stops the loop;
.B +
and
.B -
change the tempo by one.
Changes take effect as the next steps are played.
Type
.B s
to write the pattern,
.B q
to quit, or
.B Q
to discard unsaved changes and quit.
Saving writes the four rows and tempo in canonical order.
.PP
The editor normally plays through
.B games/opl3
and
.BR /dev/audio .
With
.B -p
it writes the played steps to standard output.
The stream includes live edits and omits time spent stopped.
To listen and record the same performance:
.EX
beat -ep rhythm | games/opl3 | tee /tmp/take.pcm >/dev/audio
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/beat
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR opl3 (1),
.IR audio (1)
